  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Smoke continues to impact the region, though the areas most\n  impacted shift south. \n\n- Warming, drying trend continues through the week.\n\n- Breezy conditions at the end of the week pose a fire weather\n  risk starting Friday. \n\nHigh resolution models continue to show smoke impacts for the\nregion, though conditions will improve slightly. Wildland fires\nupstream in Washington and Oregon have been less active the last\ncouple of days, and as a result there is less smoke being\nproduced. Also, a northerly component to the wind means that the\nmore concentrated smoke will be focused more to the south, mainly\nsouth of I-90. That being said, local fires are still producing\nsmoke, so no area is really in the clear. \n\nThe warming and drying trend continues through the week with\nFriday expected to be the hottest day of the week. Models are\nagreeing on a flattening of the ridge over the region late Friday\ninto next weekend. This will proved some relief from the hot\ntemperatures, and increased breezes will help ventilate some smoke\nout of the region. However, the breezy conditions are also a\nconcern for wildland fires, especially after a long, hot, dry\nweek.",
